{"name":"Urban Pigeon","hex":"#9dacb7","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"urban","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, pertaining to, characteristic of, or happening or located in, a city or town; of, pertaining to, or characteristic of life in such a place, especially when contrasted with the countryside.","Living in a city or town."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/urban"},{"term":"pigeon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of several birds of the family Columbidae within the order Columbiformes.","The meat from this bird."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To deceive with a confidence game."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pigeon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Feral pigeon","blocks":[{"text":"Feral pigeons are birds derived from domesticated populations of the rock dove, descendants that have escaped and are living independently from humans. They are sometimes given the scientific names \"Columba livia domestica\" or \"C. l. urbana\", but neither of these subspecific names is accepted by any of the ornithological authorities like the IOC World Bird List or BirdLife International. They are also called city doves, city pigeons, or street pigeons, and skemmies. Wild rock doves, domestic pigeons, and feral pigeons are all the same species and will readily interbreed. Many domestic birds have been lost, escaped or been released over the years, and these gave rise to populations of feral pigeons."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Golab_miejski.jpg/330px-Golab_miejski.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feral_pigeon","wikidata":"Q1942487"},{"title":"Columbidae","blocks":[{"text":"Columbidae is a bird family consisting of doves and pigeons. It is the only family in the order Columbiformes. These are stout-bodied birds with small heads, relatively short necks and slender bills that in some species feature fleshy ceres. They feed largely on plant matter, feeding on seeds (granivory), fruit (frugivory), and foliage (folivory)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Treron_vernans_male_-_Kent_Ridge_Park.jpg/330px-Treron_vernans_male_-_Kent_Ridge_Park.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columbidae","wikidata":"Q10856"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Columba_livia_J1.jpg/500px-Columba_livia_J1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Columba_livia_J1.jpg/1280px-Columba_livia_J1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Columba_livia_J1.jpg/500px-Columba_livia_J1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AColumba_livia_J1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Holub%2C_Lede%C4%8D_nad_S%C3%A1zavou_2019_02.jpg/500px-Holub%2C_Lede%C4%8D_nad_S%C3%A1zavou_2019_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Holub%2C_Lede%C4%8D_nad_S%C3%A1zavou_2019_02.jpg/1280px-Holub%2C_Lede%C4%8D_nad_S%C3%A1zavou_2019_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Holub%2C_Lede%C4%8D_nad_S%C3%A1zavou_2019_02.jpg/500px-Holub%2C_Lede%C4%8D_nad_S%C3%A1zavou_2019_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHolub%2C_Lede%C4%8D_nad_S%C3%A1zavou_2019_02.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Red_columba_livia.JPG/50